You can retire a name for yourself and still hear it every time you go home.

There’s the name you use privately to make sense of who you are. There’s the story you tell other people so they’ll know where you’re coming from. And there’s the name they give you based on what happened between you. Even two people who love you can know you by different names. To one, you’re the man who always came back. To another, you’re the man who was always leaving. They remember opposite ends of the same trip.

And a name affords something. Being called the strong one affords trust and responsibility. It can also make it harder to ask for help, because everyone has gotten used to telling the story with you in that role.

That’s another kind of dark. You can hear what people call you. You rarely hear the story that makes the name true to them.